WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.416
Our goal: from camera input alone. guarantee
the robot is always at a complete stop

2
00:00:04.416 --> 00:00:06.118
before it could touch the human.

3
00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:12.920
Cameras drop frames. Predictions go wrong.
That is a failure. It turns dangerous

4
00:00:12.920 --> 00:00:17.451
only if the robot then touches the person.
Standards cap dangerous failures at one

5
00:00:17.451 --> 00:00:21.817
per million hours: one in a hundred and
fourteen years of round-the-clock work.

6
00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:27.912
It starts with two views. An adapted YOLO
twenty-six returns. for every joint. not a

7
00:00:27.912 --> 00:00:29.316
pixel. but a covariance.

8
00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:34.824
Uncertainty-aware triangulation lifts
pose and covariance into three dimensions.

9
00:00:34.824 --> 00:00:36.860
Every joint arrives as an ellipsoid.

10
00:00:39.300 --> 00:00:43.659
A transformer reads two seconds of this
uncertain history. and predicts the next

11
00:00:43.659 --> 00:00:46.057
four hundred milliseconds. with covariances.

12
00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:55.937
But a learned covariance is a guess. not
a guarantee. So we calibrate: on held-out

13
00:00:55.937 --> 00:01:01.011
data we measure how far the truth lands.
in units of predicted sigma. Its 99.99th

14
00:01:01.011 --> 00:01:06.274
percentile scales every sphere into a
conformal prediction set: a ball that contains

15
00:01:06.274 --> 00:01:09.155
the true joint with 99.99
percent probability.

16
00:01:15.800 --> 00:01:20.816
The spheres become capsules: the human's
reachable occupancy. SARA shield intersects

17
00:01:20.816 --> 00:01:24.995
them with the robot's. No intersection.
go. Otherwise. fail-safe stop.

18
00:01:30.500 --> 00:01:34.470
Conformal guarantees assume tomorrow
looks like yesterday. A sketched-Lanczos

19
00:01:34.470 --> 00:01:38.492
monitor watches the networks' gradients.
and out of distribution. we reuse the

20
00:01:38.492 --> 00:01:40.554
previous prediction instead of guessing.

21
00:01:42.500 --> 00:01:47.415
Orange is the constant-velocity model of
ISO thirteen eight fifty-five. Blue is

22
00:01:47.415 --> 00:01:51.335
ours: the same confidence. in
seven point six times less space.

23
00:01:51.835 --> 00:01:56.496
Smaller is only worth having if it stays
safe. So we take every prediction failure

24
00:01:56.496 --> 00:02:00.588
and drop it around the robot: random
place in a ten-meter circle. random

25
00:02:00.588 --> 00:02:03.828
orientation. random phase of
a pick-and-place trajectory.

26
00:02:04.335 --> 00:02:09.117
Almost none of those placements can reach
the robot; two geometric filters prove it.

27
00:02:09.117 --> 00:02:13.159
In total we check two hundred sixty-five
billion placements on the GPU.

28
00:02:13.659 --> 00:02:18.250
Four of those placements ended in contact
although the shield had verified the

29
00:02:18.250 --> 00:02:22.959
trajectory. Clopper-Pearson on both factors
bounds the dangerous failure rate at

30
00:02:22.959 --> 00:02:27.315
nine point five times ten to the minus
seven per hour. with 99.999 percent

31
00:02:27.315 --> 00:02:27.962
confidence.

32
00:02:28.462 --> 00:02:32.754
On hardware: a Franka arm. a RealSense
camera. the same pipeline at twenty-five

33
00:02:32.754 --> 00:02:37.155
hertz. Blue is the human's conformal
occupancy over the robot's stopping horizon.

34
00:02:37.155 --> 00:02:41.719
When it meets the robot's reachable set.
the shield brakes. In every tested instance

35
00:02:41.719 --> 00:02:45.087
the robot stood still long before
the operator could reach it.

36
00:02:49.462 --> 00:02:52.620
Vision-based perception. with a
number you can certify against.
